# [JOSE](../wg/jose.html)

## Summary

The JOSE Working Group met at IETF 127. The session included status updates on active working group specifications, discussions regarding post-quantum and hybrid cryptography registrations, JSON Web Proofs (JWP), modular BBS sub-proofs, and operational considerations for post-quantum migration and key discovery.

Key outcomes include proceeding to Working Group Last Call (WGLC) for `draft-ietf-jose-hpke-pq-pqt` and preparing `draft-ietf-jose-pq-composite-sigs` for WGLC following minor updates to algorithm listings and JOSE-specific use case clarifications.

---

## Key Discussion Points

### Working Group Status & General Updates
* Chairs presented the [JOSE WG Chair Slides](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-jose-jose-wg-chair-slides-00).
* An update was provided on the draft deprecating the `none` algorithm (now in the RFC Editor queue). During IESG review, the document was updated so that it updates both RFC 7518 and RFC 7519 for consistency across specifications.
* A pending liaison statement has been finalized and will be sent out to the working group mailing list this week.

### JOSE HPKE PQ & PQ/T Algorithm Registrations
* **Slides:** [JOSE HPKE PQ & PQ/T Algorithm Registrations](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-jose-jose-hpke-pq-pqt-algorithm-registrations-00)
* **Draft:** `draft-ietf-jose-hpke-pq-pqt`
* **Presenter:** Brian Campbell (on behalf of Filip Skokan and co-authors)
* **Discussion:**
  * Draft version `-02` maintains 5 cipher suites and 10 total JOSE `alg` identifiers utilizing SHAKE256 KDF and AES-256-GCM AEAD for HPKE.
  * Deployment guidance was updated to remain neutral on preferring pure post-quantum (PQ) versus post-quantum/traditional (PQ/T) hybrids.
  * Broader updates to implementation requirement levels in the JOSE algorithms registry are deferred to future work; all registrations in this draft remain `optional`.
  * Implementation challenges in browser environments were discussed (e.g., WebCrypto API exposure for SHAKE256 KDF vs HKDF-SHA256).
  * Area Director Deb Cooley noted external dependencies in CFRG (CFRG HPKE PQ and KEM combiners drafts) that are not yet finished.
  * Filip Skokan, Sophie Schmieg, John Bradley, and Brian Campbell supported advancing the document through WGLC now and seeking early IANA registrations, noting that the parameters have been stable across implementations for a prolonged period.

### PQ/T Hybrid Composite Signatures for JOSE and COSE
* **Slides:** [PQ/T Hybrid Composite Signatures for JOSE and COSE](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-jose-pqt-hybrid-composite-signatures-for-jose-and-cose-00)
* **Draft:** `draft-ietf-jose-pq-composite-sigs`
* **Discussion:**
  * The draft normatively references the LAMPS composite RFCs for key generation, signing, and verification.
  * The specification aligns with LAMPS encodings by using ASN.1 DER for ECDSA components to maintain a single format across X.509 certificates and TLS, while retaining raw byte values for ML-DSA keys and signatures (`AKP` key type per RFC 9964).
  * The authors proposed trimming the list of supported algorithm combinations down to 5 core combinations (including an ECDSA P-384 / ML-DSA combination requested for WebAuthn/FIDO).
  * John Bradley, Sophie Schmieg, John Gray, and Mike Jones discussed the decision to omit P-521 (ES512) combinations, citing performance penalties and lack of deployment demand.
  * Filip Skokan asked for clarification when new algorithm requests are brought forward regarding whether code points are needed for JOSE, COSE, or both.

### BBS and Modular Sub-proofs with JSON Web Proofs
* **Slides:** [BBS and Modular Sub-proofs with JSON Web Proofs](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-jose-bbs-and-modular-sub-proofs-with-json-web-proofs-00)
* **Draft:** `draft-ietf-jose-jwp-modular-bbs`
* **Presenter:** Brent Zundel
* **Discussion:**
  * The draft was formally adopted by the WG.
  * Recent changes in the underlying CFRG BBS blind signature specification now support raw scalars, allowing simplification of definitions in this draft while maintaining signaling for raw versus hashed scalar values (to prevent invalid range proofs over hashed values).
  * Key binding is proposed to be generalized as a standard claim taking multiple messages rather than a specialized mechanism.
  * The authors solicited feedback on whether to support SD-CWT / CBOR serializations alongside SD-JWT formats.
  * On X.509 key discovery for BLS public keys, John Bradley suggested defining ASN.1 encodings within the CFRG BLS curve specifications rather than within this draft.

### JSON Web Proof (JWP) Family
* **Slides:** [IETF 127 JSON Web Proof](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-jose-ietf-127-json-web-proof-00)
* **Drafts:** `draft-ietf-jose-json-web-proof`, `draft-ietf-jose-json-proof-token`, `draft-ietf-jose-json-proof-algorithms`
* **Presenter:** Mike Jones
* **Discussion:**
  * Recent draft updates include alignment with `draft-ietf-jose-json-proof-algorithms` for fully specified algorithm definitions, updated CBOR concise document notation, and synchronization with OpenID Connect CWT specifications.
  * Status update on CFRG dependencies: The core CFRG BBS signature specification is expected to enter CFRG WGLC shortly, while the blind and pseudonym drafts are entering crypto panel review.

### Any Other Business: Post-Quantum Migration & JWKS Agility
* Breno de Medeiros highlighted work on OAuth/OIDC crypto agility (`draft-demedeiros-oidc-crypto-agility`), focusing on issues where client libraries fail or crash when encountering unrecognized key types (e.g., ML-DSA / AKP keys) inside JSON Web Key Sets (JWKS).
* Brian Campbell, John Bradley, Mike Jones, Filip Skokan, and Kartik discussed practical migration risks, JWKS key set pollution, and the necessity of preventing rollback attacks while facilitating transitional deployments.

